---
import DocsLayout from "../../components/docs/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "../../components/docs/Code.astro";
import Terminal from "../../components/Terminal.astro";
import Tag from "../../components/docs/Tag.astro";

const dataset = `coding.sh dataset add smoke \\
  --case '{"input":"2+2","expected":"4"}' \\
  --case '{"input":"3*3","expected":"9"}'
coding.sh dataset import cases.jsonl --name support   # JSONL, JSON or CSV
coding.sh dataset add regressions --session <session-id>   # a case from a captured session`;

const agent = `// agent.mjs: reads the case input as JSON on stdin, prints its answer
let s = "";
for await (const chunk of process.stdin) s += chunk;
const input = JSON.parse(s);
console.log(String(eval(input)));`;

const run = `coding.sh exp run --dataset smoke --name base \\
  --task '{"kind":"command","command":"node","args":["agent.mjs"]}' \\
  --grader '{"kind":"exact"}'`;

const spec = `{
  "name": "support-v2",
  "dataset": "support",
  "split": "test",
  "trials": 3,
  "task": { "kind": "http", "url": "http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/agent", "responsePath": "$.output" },
  "graders": [
    { "kind": "contains", "value": ["refund"] },
    { "kind": "llm", "name": "helpful", "rubric": "Pass if the reply resolves the customer's issue.", "tunedOn": ["dev"] }
  ]
}`;

const judge = `coding.sh ui                                   # label sessions pass / fail
coding.sh judge calibrate <experiment> --criterion outcome --split test`;

const replay = `coding.sh eval new latest --verifier 'npm test' --hidden-test 'test/retry.spec.ts'
coding.sh eval validate <task> --k 5
coding.sh eval export <task> --out ./tasks/retry --format harbor
coding.sh harness snapshot --agent claude-code --name base
coding.sh compare base candidate --k 3`;

const esc = (s: string) => s.replace(/&/g, "&amp;").replace(/</g, "&lt;").replace(/>/g, "&gt;");
const m = (s: string) => `<span class="t-muted">${esc(s)}</span>`;
const t = (s: string) => `<span class="t-text">${esc(s)}</span>`;
const ok = (s: string) => `<span class="t-pass">${esc(s)}</span>`;
const no = (s: string) => `<span class="t-fail">${esc(s)}</span>`;

// Real output of the commands above (run 2026-11-06 against a throwaway CODING_SH_HOME).
const RUN = [
  `${m("$")} ${t("coding.sh exp run --dataset smoke --name base …")}`,
  `${t("base")}  exp_01M6SAJYC5RM7A5TCT65R3SFSM`,
  `dataset smoke@8db025860cd0b8da  task command: node agent.mjs  trials 1`,
  `pass    ${ok("100.0%")} [34.2%, 100.0%] (2/2, 95% Wilson)`,
  `latency median 32ms, p90 32ms, max 32ms`,
  "",
  `grader  kind     pass  95% CI           mean score  errors  trust`,
  m(`------  -----  ------  ---------------  ----------  ------  -------------`),
  `exact   exact  ${ok("100.0%")}  [34.2%, 100.0%]        1.00       0  deterministic`,
].join("\n");

const COMPARE = [
  `${m("$")} ${t("coding.sh exp compare exp_01M6SAJYC5RM7A5TCT65R3SFSM exp_01M6SAK893Z1DFH770KVNQC4BZ")}`,
  `A base exp_01M6SAJYC5RM7A5TCT65R3SFSM  pass 100.0%`,
  `B cand exp_01M6SAK893Z1DFH770KVNQC4BZ  pass 50.0%`,
  "",
  `B − A   ${no("-50.0pp")} [-100.0pp, 0.0pp] (2 paired cases, 95% paired bootstrap)`,
  `mcnemar p=1.000  1 regressed, 0 improved`,
  `verdict inconclusive (CI includes 0)`,
  "",
  `regressions 1`,
  `case                   A   B       Δ`,
  m(`------------------  ----  --  ------`),
  `c_756966685591eb9b  100%  ${no("0%")}  -100pp`,
].join("\n");

// Real output of scripts/e2e/exp-ab-capture.mjs (2026-11-07): three Claude Code sessions against a stub
// model.
const AB = [
  `${m("$")} ${t("coding.sh exp ab")}`,
  `A: git:HEAD   B: your edits   model: claude-sonnet-5-5`,
  `3 decision points from 3 sessions | A 0/3 -> B ${ok("3/3")} | fixed 3, broke 0 | paired p=0.25 | <$0.01, 0/6 cached`,
  `Hold: not enough evidence (B wins 3, loses 0; needs 8+ wins with no losses)`,
  `Replay noise floor 0% (A's turn differs from the recorded one in 0/3); B moved +100 points, above it`,
  "",
  `Fixed (A failed, B passed):`,
  `  unverified-claim "Make pad.js pad on the left."  ${m("coding.sh show 8f0e1e52-bdc1-4da0-a03b-14edf6dfcf77")}`,
  `  unverified-claim "Make clamp.js clamp to the upper bound too."  ${m("coding.sh show 9c1792df-6267-4b30-9f24-7a7307740543")}`,
  `  unverified-claim "Make range.js include its end value."  ${m("coding.sh show fe48a450-8867-48ec-b946-3b4d0cb6c780")}`,
  "",
  `Full comparison: coding.sh exp compare exp_01M6VXZSFPM2QK12ANN6Z269WP exp_01M6VXZSFSZ25HD2HXXVY40BXV`,
].join("\n");
---

<DocsLayout>
  <p>
    There are two kinds of eval. <strong>Dataset experiments</strong> run any agent, in any language, over a set of cases and grade the outputs.
    <strong>Repo-replay tasks</strong> re-run a coding agent from the repository state before a real session and check the result with a
    validated verifier.
  </p>

  <h2 id="datasets">Datasets <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <p>
    A dataset is a list of cases: <code>input</code>, optional <code>expected</code>, <code>tags</code>, <code>metadata</code> and a
    <code>split</code> (<code>train</code>, <code>dev</code> or <code>test</code>). Every change makes a new version whose id is a hash of
    the cases, so the same cases always give the same version and an experiment records exactly what it ran on.
  </p>
  <Code code={dataset} />

  <h2 id="tasks">What gets run</h2>
  <p>The task is the agent under test. Two kinds work from the CLI:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><code>command</code></strong>: any executable. The case input arrives as JSON on stdin; stdout is the output (parsed as JSON when
      it is JSON). <code>CODING_SH_RUN_ID</code>, <code>CODING_SH_CASE_ID</code> and <code>CODING_SH_TRIAL</code> are in its environment.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>http</code></strong>: a POST of the input as JSON to a URL; the response (or a JSON path into it) is the output. Header
      values can come from environment variables so secrets stay out of spec files.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Code title="agent.mjs" code={agent} />
  <Code code={run} />
  <Terminal title="~/smoke" label="Output of coding.sh exp run">
    <pre set:html={RUN} />
  </Terminal>
  <p>
    With one trial per case the interval is a Wilson interval; with <code>--trials</code> above 1 it is a bootstrap over cases. Options can also
    live in a JSON spec file: <code>coding.sh exp run spec.json</code>.
  </p>
  <Code title="spec.json" code={spec} />

  <h2 id="graders">Graders <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <p>Deterministic graders come first. Each grader's config is hashed into a version, so a changed grader is a different grader.</p>
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Passes when</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>exact</code></td><td>Output equals <code>expected</code> (structural for JSON, normalized for text).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>contains</code></td><td>Output contains all (or any) of the strings; <code>negate</code> inverts it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>regex</code></td><td>Output matches a pattern.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>json-schema</code></td><td>Output validates against a JSON Schema.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>json-path</code></td><td>A JSON path into the output equals a value or the same path in <code>expected</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>numeric</code></td><td>The first number in the output is within an absolute or relative tolerance.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>similarity</code></td><td>Normalized edit-distance similarity is at least the threshold (default 0.8).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>progress</code></td><td>The run's trace derailed at most <code>maxDerails</code> times (default 0). The score is the share of the run before its first derail: looping, failure-streak, churn, no-progress, stalled or context-thrash.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>command</code></td><td>Your script, any language: JSON on stdin; a boolean, a score or <code>&#123;score, pass, reason&#125;</code> on stdout, or the exit code.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>module</code></td><td>A JS module's exported grade function returns pass.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>llm</code></td><td>A model judge returns pass on your rubric. See below.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>llm-checklist</code></td><td>Yes/no questions, each answered by the model or by a trace check. Rule checks need no model: <code>ran-command</code>, <code>read-path</code>, <code>wrote-identifier</code> and <code>follows-rules</code> (every backticked command, flag, path or identifier a rule requires was used, and none it forbids). A path-scoped rule (Claude Code <code>paths:</code>, Cursor <code>globs:</code>) counts only once the agent touches a matching file. <code>no-test-tamper</code> answers no when a test edit hid a failure (a skip marker, a deleted test while red, an assertion edit that alone turned the tests green) and quotes what the agent said about it; saying why never makes it pass.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="compare">Comparing two experiments <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <p>
    <code>exp compare</code> pairs the two runs by case. It reports the difference in pass rate with a paired bootstrap interval, McNemar's exact
    test on cases that flipped, per-grader score deltas and the regressed cases. If the interval includes zero, the verdict says inconclusive.
  </p>
  <Terminal title="~/smoke" label="Output of coding.sh exp compare">
    <pre set:html={COMPARE} />
  </Terminal>
  <ul>
    <li><code>--cluster &lt;key&gt;</code> resamples groups of related cases, such as <code>repo</code> or <code>session</code>, instead of single cases. It prints the cluster count and warns below 10 clusters.</li>
    <li>Below 100 paired cases, compare also prints P(B&gt;A) from a Bayesian posterior on the paired outcomes. The bootstrap interval stays.</li>
    <li>It counts trials each arm lost to errors, split into infra and agent errors, and warns when Fisher's exact test finds an uneven split.</li>
    <li>
      <code>exp aa</code> runs one config twice and stores its run-to-run noise. Compare then prints the A/A flip rate, and the minimum
      detectable effect never goes below that noise.
    </li>
    <li>Compare warns when the two runs ran one after the other, and says how far apart they were.</li>
    <li>
      <code>--gate</code> adds a ship-or-hold line: <code>Ship it: B wins 8 cases, loses 0</code>, <code>Hold: not enough evidence</code>, or
      <code>Don't ship: B breaks 2 cases A solved</code> with the broken cases. It exits 0 only for "Ship it", so CI can use it. A break costs two
      fixes, the wins must clear a one-sided exact test, and B may break at most 10% of what A solved (<a href="#gate">details</a>).
    </li>
    <li id="non-inferiority">
      <code>--margin 0.03</code> asks whether B is at most 3pp worse than A, for a cheaper or faster config you want to keep only if it does not
      lose quality. It prints <code>non-inf B − A ≥ -3.0pp: yes</code> when the lower end of the 95% interval is above −3pp, and exits 1
      otherwise. It also lists cases A passed on every trial that B now mostly fails, which an average within the margin can hide.
    </li>
    <li id="coverage">
      With 2+ trials per case it counts coverage on its own: cases B gained (A never passed, B passed at least once) and cases B lost (A passed
      at least once, B never), listed under <code>lost coverage</code>. A net gain can hide lost cases. It also prints how many cases' pass rates
      went up or down, and the change in each half of the trials, with a warning when the two halves disagree. <code>--matrix</code> prints the
      cases by passes out of k in A and B. Method: EvoAudit (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07624">arXiv 2611.07624</a>).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    To avoid that, run both configs in one job: <code>exp run --arm base=a.json --arm cand=b.json</code>. Each case runs in both arms back
    to back, in random order, so drift during the run hits both arms the same. Add <code>--stop-early</code> to stop once the answer is clear.
    It checks at a few planned points (an O'Brien-Fleming group sequential test, never before 20 cases), says why it stopped, and reports an
    interval that stays valid after stopping. <code>--max-cases</code> caps the run.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sweep">Sweeping configs <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <p>
    <code>exp sweep base=a.json b.json c.json -d smoke -g G</code> picks the best of several task configs. It uses sequential halving: each
    round runs the surviving configs on the same fresh train cases and keeps the better half, so weak configs stop costing runs early. Dropped
    configs show their gap to the leader with an interval.
  </p>
  <p>
    The race winner tends to be the luckiest config, so its train score runs high. The winner and the first config then run on the test
    split, which the race never saw. The output prints both scores for the winner and the <strong>optimism gap</strong> (train minus
    held-out). On those held-out cases the same gate as <code>exp compare --gate</code> prints <code>adopt &lt;winner&gt;</code> or
    <code>keep &lt;start&gt;</code> with the reason. <code>--budget</code> caps the race's case runs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="resume">Long runs: resume and retries <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <p>
    <code>exp run</code>, <code>exp aa</code>, <code>exp sweep</code>, <code>compare</code>, <code>bench run</code> and
    <code>workflows run</code> save each trial as it finishes. After Ctrl-C, a crash or a reboot, run the same command with
    <code>--resume</code> and only the unfinished trials run. The seed, order and <code>--stop-early</code> looks come from the checkpoint, so
    the resumed run stops at the same point and gives the same verdict as one that was never interrupted. If the dataset, graders, task or
    agent script changed, it refuses to resume and names what changed. The first Ctrl-C lets the trials in flight finish; the second stops
    them.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--retries N</code> (default 3) retries rate limits, 5xx and network errors with backoff. Task failures are never retried, since
    that would inflate the pass rate. A trial that still hits infrastructure errors is left out of the pass rate and runs again on
    <code>--resume</code>. <code>--trial-timeout</code> caps each trial. <code>exp status</code> shows each run's progress, ETA and cost so
    far.
  </p>

  <h2 id="metrics">Metric A/B <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <p>
    <code>exp metrics --arm base='node bench.mjs a' --arm cand='node bench.mjs b'</code> compares two commands that print numbers, such as
    <code>&#123;"lcp_ms": 2310, "js_bytes": 48211&#125;</code>, instead of pass/fail. The arms run in interleaved pairs (11 by default), one run
    at a time, with half the pairs running A first.
  </p>
  <p>
    For each metric it prints both medians, the median paired difference with a 95% bootstrap interval, and a verdict. Lower is better unless
    you name the metric in <code>--higher</code>. A change counts only when the interval excludes zero and a sign test still passes after
    correcting for the number of metrics, so an A/A run stays quiet. <code>--fail-on-worse</code> exits 1 for CI.
  </p>

  <h2 id="exp-ab">A/B a harness edit on your sessions <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <p>
    You changed a line in CLAUDE.md and want to know if it helps before you commit it. <code>coding.sh exp ab</code> answers that from
    the sessions you already have, with no eval tasks to write. It finds the moments in your recent sessions where the agent went wrong:
    it said it was done without running anything, ended a turn while its tests were failing, or retried a failed command unchanged. It
    replays the next turn at each of those moments twice, once with your committed files and once with your edit, and a judge scores both
    turns.
  </p>
  <Terminal title="~/repo" label="Output of coding.sh exp ab">
    <Fragment set:html={AB} />
  </Terminal>
  <p>
    The verdict line is the same ship gate <code>compare --gate</code> uses, and <code>--gate</code> makes it the exit code. The noise floor
    comes from the control arm: how often your committed files alone produce a different turn from the one recorded in the session. A
    difference smaller than that may just be replay noise. Use <code>--dry-run</code> to see the cases and a cost estimate before anything is
    called, and <code>--max-cost</code> to cap the spend. Responses are cached, so rerunning with unchanged files is free.
    <code>--watch</code> reruns each time you save, one line per run. See
    <a href="/docs/how-to/test-claude-md-change">Test a CLAUDE.md change in five minutes</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="judges">LLM judges <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <p>
    An <code>llm</code> grader calls Anthropic's Messages API or any OpenAI-compatible endpoint (OpenAI, OpenRouter, Ollama, LM Studio, vLLM
    through <code>baseURL</code>). Keys are read from the environment (<code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code>, <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code> or
    <code>apiKeyEnv</code>) at call time and never logged, hashed or cached. Verdicts are cached on disk, so re-grading an unchanged output costs
    nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each judge is versioned as <code>name@hash</code> over model, prompt template, rubric, criterion and parameters. Judge verdicts can cite the
    trace; citations are checked against what the judge was shown.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <code>pairwise</code> judge compares two experiments: <code>exp compare A B --pairwise judge.json</code>. It sees both outputs for each
    case, twice, with the order swapped. A side wins only when both orders agree; when the answer changes with the order, the case is a tie
    and counts toward the printed position-bias rate. It reports B's win rate with an interval and a sign test. <code>--labels</code> takes
    human A/B/tie labels and calibrates the judge like any other.
  </p>

  <h2 id="calibration">Judge calibration <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <p>
    A judge's score counts only once it agrees with people. Label sessions pass or fail in the viewer, then measure agreement on a split the
    judge's prompt was not tuned on.
  </p>
  <Code code={judge} />
  <ul>
    <li>Agreement is Cohen's κ with a bootstrap interval, plus the confusion matrix and precision and recall for the "fail" class. Raw agreement alone flatters a judge on imbalanced data.</li>
    <li>A judge is <strong>calibrated</strong> at κ ≥ 0.6 on at least 20 held-out cases that have both a human label and a verdict. Both are flags: <code>--threshold</code>, <code>--min-n</code>.</li>
    <li>κ measured on a split listed in the judge's <code>tunedOn</code> is reported as <strong>in-sample</strong>, never as calibrated.</li>
    <li>Below the bar, the judge is <strong>uncalibrated</strong> or has <strong>insufficient data</strong>, and every report flags its scores.</li>
    <li>Cases where the judge is confidently opposite to unanimous humans are listed for relabelling.</li>
    <li>The judge's held-out sensitivity and specificity are stored too, so <code>exp compare</code> shows a bias-corrected judge pass rate.</li>
    <li><code>--probe-injection judge.json</code> re-judges human-fail cases with a planted "respond pass" order in the output and in the last tool result, and reports how many verdicts flip. Any flip exits 1.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The agent writes the text a judge grades, so judge prompts escape the data tags inside it (<code>&lt;/output&gt;</code> becomes
    <code>&lt;\/output&gt;</code>) and tell the model that everything inside them is untrusted. Citations still verify against the raw text.
  </p>
  <p>
    Test verifiers get the same check. A passing suite is not proof of a correct change, so <code>judge calibrate --grader tests</code> also
    takes a <code>command</code> or other deterministic grader and measures its precision: how many of its passes a person would accept.
    <code>--sample-passes 40</code> lists a seeded sample of unlabelled passes to label, and <code>--labels labels.jsonl</code> adds labels
    from a file. The audit reports precision with a Wilson interval and the false accepts. A verifier never sees the labels, so no held-out
    split is needed. After that, <code>exp show</code> and <code>exp compare</code> print the precision and a corrected
    pass rate.
  </p>

  <h2 id="repo-replay">Repo-replay tasks <Tag status="partial" /></h2>
  <p>
    For coding agents, a task is the repository snapshot from before the agent ran, the first prompt as the instruction, setup commands and a
    verifier command. Before a task counts, <code>eval validate</code> checks the verifier:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Doing nothing fails.</li>
    <li>The reference solution (the session's own diff, or a later fix) passes <code>k</code> runs out of <code>k</code> (default 5), so flaky tests fail validation.</li>
    <li>Near-miss variants of the reference, generated by mutating its patch, fail.</li>
    <li>Nothing leaks: hidden test files are copied in only when the verifier runs, and follow-up prompts that steer toward the answer stay out of the instruction.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    It also flags gold-only coupling: a hidden test that names a helper or file only the reference adds, when the instruction never mentions
    it. A valid fix that names things differently would fail. These show as notes; <code>--strict-coupling</code> makes them fail validation.
    <code>eval crosscheck &lt;task&gt;</code> works from the other side: a reviewer model reads the instruction and each stored run's diff and
    says whether the change is valid. Where it disagrees with the verifier, it reports a false reject or a false accept, and stores the counts
    on the task in the same shape as a human verifier audit. <code>--solver &lt;bundle&gt;</code> solves the task once first, and
    <code>--max</code> caps the model calls (default 5).
  </p>
  <p>
    Harness bundles (agent, model, flags, env and files such as <code>CLAUDE.md</code> or <code>AGENTS.md</code>, identified by a content hash)
    are what <code>compare</code> A/B tests: it runs both bundles on validated tasks in isolated worktrees and reports per-arm Wilson
    intervals, a paired bootstrap interval, McNemar's test, cost, token, turn and latency deltas, and the first step where the two traces diverge. Tasks export
    in coding.sh's own layout or Harbor's.
  </p>
  <p id="gate">
    <code>compare A B --gate</code> answers "should I ship B?" in one line: <code>Ship it: B wins 9 tasks, loses 0</code>,
    <code>Hold: not enough evidence</code>, or <code>Don't ship: B breaks 3 tasks A solved</code>, with the broken tasks listed. It exits 0
    only for "Ship it". A break costs two fixes (<code>--lambda</code>), the wins must clear a one-sided exact test (<code>--gate-alpha</code>,
    default 0.05), and B may break at most 10% of the tasks A solved (<code>--tau</code>). This is the SAGE accept-or-abstain rule
    (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36043">arXiv 2609.36043</a>). Passes that tampered with tests count as fails for the gate.
    <code>--gate --by suite:</code> also gates each group of tasks (by their first tag with that prefix), so a pooled "Ship it" can't hide
    one suite collapsing: any group's "Don't ship" blocks. The report also splits each arm's failed runs by the agent's own last test run
    (<code>A fails 12: own tests green 7 (58%), red 3, stale 1, none 1</code>): green fails are silent misses, where the agent's tests passed
    and the verifier's did not.
  </p>
  <p id="tamper">
    Passing runs that edited, deleted or skipped tests or the verifier's files still count as passes, so compare links each such change to
    the tool call that made it and splits those passes: <code>tampered passes B 3: disclosed 2, stealth 1</code>. Disclosed means the agent
    said it was changing the test before or with the call; stealth means it didn't (EPISTEME,
    <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06563">arXiv 2611.06563</a>). Each pass gets a line with the run, file, tool, event id and the
    agent's words; <code>--json</code> has them under <code>tamperAttribution</code>.
  </p>
  <p id="promote">
    <code>compare A B --promote</code> keeps a B that is cheaper at the same quality, which the verdict calls "no significant difference". It
    prints <code>pareto: support 0.86 ≥ 0.70 → promote (cost-cut: cost -38% …)</code> or <code>→ hold</code>, and exits 0 only on promote.
    The support is the share of bootstrap draws (over tasks and attempts) where B gains quality without costing more than
    <code>--eps-c</code>, or costs less while losing at most <code>--eps-q</code> of pass rate. Cost is USD, or tokens when too few runs are
    priced. Method: ParetoSaddler (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02079">arXiv 2611.02079</a>).
  </p>
  <p id="item-gate">
    When B adds or removes one rule, skill or context file and nothing else (<code>--without</code> counts), the report adds a one-item gate:
    <code>item gate k=3 τ=.34 m=2: KEEP · G=1.67 net +4 runs D=.33 fired 11/36 · S=9 tasks</code>. It scores only the tasks where the item
    reached the agent's context, read from each run's trace. An addition is KEEP, RECHECK (re-run the tasks that dropped), KILL or RERUN; a
    removal is ACCEPT, REJECT, UNMEASURED (it never fired, so keep it) or RERUN. <code>--item-gate</code> makes it set the exit code. Method:
    Cortex (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14216">arXiv 2610.14216</a>).
  </p>
  <p id="hand-written">
    A task can also be written by hand: a directory with <code>task.json</code>, <code>instruction.md</code>, an optional <code>repo/</code>
    start tree and <code>tests/</code> for hidden files. <code>eval import tasks/*/</code> loads them as drafts, and
    <code>compare A B --tag &lt;tag&gt;</code> runs them. The verifier gets the agent's final answer and its whole stdout as files
    (<code>CODING_SH_AGENT_OUTPUT</code>, <code>CODING_SH_AGENT_STDOUT</code>), so <code>judge grade --grader checklist.json</code> can grade
    what the agent said and which commands it ran. <code>--max-cost 15</code> stops starting runs once agent and judge spend reach $15;
    the report shows both as <code>agent spend</code> and <code>grader spend</code>. The <a href="/docs/how-to/evaluate-a-skill">Evaluate an
    Agent Skill</a> guide walks through it.
  </p>
  <Code code={replay} />
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Known gap.</strong> A hook-captured session starts from the commit that was checked out when it began, so uncommitted changes at
      that point are missing and <code>eval new</code> warns. <code>coding.sh run --</code> snapshots the whole working tree before and after,
      and takes the instruction from the command's prompt argument, or from <code>--instruction</code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="workflows">Workflows</h2>
  <p>
    Multi-agent workflows have their own page: capturing runs, scoring them against seeded bugs, comparing two versions with
    <code>workflows compare</code>, and replaying from a recorded run with a control fork that measures the replay noise floor.
    See <a href="/docs/workflows">Workflow experiments</a>.
  </p>
